The original does a linear scan over every message definition on each call. This version builds a 65536-entry index table once, on the first call, and then answers each lookup with a single table read.

- **Same result as before:** the table is filled only from the constant profile data (`mesgs`). If two entries share a `num`, only the first one is recorded, so the function returns the same pointer the scan would have found.
- **Thread safety:** the table is a function-local static, so C++ initializes it once and safely even if several threads call at once.
- **Memory:** about 128 KB.
- **Assumption:** there are fewer than 65535 message definitions, which the profile easily meets. If that ever stopped holding, the function falls back to the original linear scan.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Index
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        bool ok;
        Index()
        {
            ok = (MESGS < 0xFFFF);
            for (int i = 0; i < 65536; i++)
                idx[i] = 0xFFFF;
            if (ok)
            {
                for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
                {
                    FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
                    if (idx[n] == 0xFFFF)
                        idx[n] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
                }
            }
        }
    };
    static const Index index;

    if (index.ok)
    {
        FIT_UINT16 i = index.idx[num];
        if (i == 0xFFFF)
            return NULL;
        return &mesgs[i];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```